# Data (raw is re-downloadable; processed is regenerable)
data/raw/*
data/processed/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep
!data/processed/.gitkeep
# Exception: walk-forward baseline predictions are load-bearing for the
# v1.0-results freeze (SARIMAX is not bit-reproducible across BLAS builds)
!data/processed/baselines/
# Same rationale: validation-window predictions determine the ensemble
# weights, so they are load-bearing for the freeze too
!data/processed/validation_preds/
# Daily-direct walk-forward predictions (RQ4's direct arm)
!data/processed/daily_direct/
# The OOD live-data cache is NOT re-downloadable: the Energy-Charts API
# returns different data every day, so the exact window behind the reported
# OOD numbers exists only here. Committing it is what makes that result
# reproducible after the v1.0-results tag.
!data/raw/live_ood_de.csv
# OOD predictions + summary, derived from that cache
!data/processed/ood/
# SHAP summaries behind thesis figures 10-15 and the 4-6 table. Small CSVs,
# and committing them means the figures can be redrawn (run_shap.py
# --figures-only) without refitting the interpretation models, which live
# under the gitignored models/ tree.
!data/processed/shap/
# LSTM seed-ensemble members and their averages, behind the supplementary
# result in reports/tables/seed_ensemble.*. Not regenerable in practice:
# retraining four LSTM walk-forwards costs hours, and the validation-window
# members are what the ensemble weights are fitted from. Committing them is
# what makes the supplementary table and tests/test_seed_ensemble.py
# reproducible from a clean clone.
!data/processed/seed_ensemble/
!data/processed/seed_ensemble_val/
# Combination-ladder result table (2026-08-20). One small CSV, and it is the
# evidence behind a thesis claim -- the null ladder -- so it is committed
# rather than left regenerable-in-principle.
!data/processed/combination_ladder/
# LEAR multi-window sweep frames (2026-08-20). The 1456 arm costs ~1 hour of
# compute to regenerate and is the evidence behind the window-averaging
# finding, so it is committed rather than left regenerable-in-principle.
!data/processed/lear_windows/

# Models & experiment artifacts
models/*
!models/.gitkeep
mlruns/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Environments & editors
.env
.venv/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# Graphify knowledge-graph output (regenerate with: graphify . --code-only)
graphify-out/

# Per-run stdout from long walk-forward jobs. Local diagnostic trail only —
# what a run concluded belongs in logs/decisions.md, which IS committed.
logs/runs/

# Claude Code: personal/session-local state (shared config in .claude/ is committed)
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/

# Pinned physical-data snapshot (Energy-Charts + EEX), ~9.7 MB.
# Committed deliberately, following the !data/raw/live_ood_de.csv precedent
# above: feature code reads ONLY these files and never the live API, so the
# snapshot is an input to the results, not a cache. A result computed against
# a live source is not reproducible, and each file's sha256 in
# provenance.json is what makes drift fail loudly instead of silently.
!data/raw/physical/

# Per-variant ablation predictions (scripts/run_full_physical_ablation.py).
# Regenerable intermediate data, cached only so an interrupted run resumes
# instead of restarting. Deliberately NOT under data/processed, which the
# frozen_results_guard hook protects.
data/ablation_cache/

# The benchmark input the FROZEN results were produced from.
#
# Committed deliberately, following the data/raw/physical/ precedent. The tags
# v1.0-results and v1.1-ood ship no benchmark data at all, so reproducing them
# depended on re-downloading from the epftoolbox server -- and if that source
# ever moves or revises its data, the frozen thesis numbers become
# unreproducible. That is exactly the failure the snapshot rule was written to
# prevent; it was applied to the physical data and not, until now, to the
# primary benchmark input.
!data/raw/DE.csv
!data/raw/provenance_benchmark.json
